[Music]  even though tuscan raiders show up in  star wars from the very beginning  and reappear throughout they remain  mysterious and poorly understood  who are they where do they come from  what do they want  and what do fans and casual viewers  alike need to know about them  the tuscan raiders appear first and most  memorably in a new hope  the sand people are initially presented  as fearsome savages  dangers to civilized people like luke  skywalker and his family  they roam the desert attacking and  pillaging the unsuspecting  when luke leaves the safety of his  homestead to search the jungle and waste  for his escape droid  r2d2 the raiders quickly overpower the  naive and unprepared young man  knocking him unconscious so they can  ransack his land speeder  luke is only saved when the mad hermit  ben kenobi  appears and scares away the raiders by  mimicking the call of the crate dragon  a beast they fear despite his ability to  scare them away  ben also warns luke the gentleman wastes  enough to be traveled lightly  of course if luke had died in the scene  he never would have blown up the death  star  fought darth vader become a jedi knight  and help defeat the empire  perhaps the trilogy would have centered  on leia organa luke's force-sensitive  sister instead  star wars mastermind george lucas  created the tuscan raiders in their  desert world to resemble the bedouins  tribes of arab nomads however it's  likely lucas's conception of the raiders  was  also influenced by the so-called savage  indians of john ford westerns  especially from the searchers which star  wars famously pays homage to  in the searchers comanche warriors  attacked the white settlers homestead  killing the family and burning the house  down while it's the imperial storm  troopers and not the tuscan raiders that  exterminate luke's family  lucas designed the scene in direct  homage to the scene from the searchers  in general lucas depicts the sand people  the way that ford depicts the comanche  brutal bloodthirsty and uncivilized in  terms of the name  tuscan sounds quite a bit like tuskegee  as in the tuskegee airmen  the first black us army airmen who  trained at the tuskegee army airfield in  alabama before flying in combat during  world war  ii lucas has long been interested in  their story even making a movie about  them with 2012's red tails  finally lucas was influenced by frank  herbert's sci-fi novel dune  which features nomad dwellers on the  desert planet arrakis  banthas are large trundling creatures  that resemble  elephants with huge droopy faces they  have curving tusks on the sides of their  heads shaped like cornucopias or perhaps  princess leia's hair buttons in a new  hope  the raiders used them as beasts of  burden lucas clearly liked the banthus  as he digitally inserted a lot more of  them in the special edition of return of  the jedi which features a herd of wild  banters roaming the tatooine desert they  are seen as jabba the hutt  transports luke han solo and company  across the dune sea to the pit of  kharkum to be slowly digested over a  thousand  years by the mighty sarlacc this is an  appropriate fate  since jabba had previously told han you  may have been a good smuggler but now  you're bantha fodder  digital panthers also appear briefly in  the phantom menace and attack of the  clones  chapter 9 of the mandalorian also  heavily features banthus  early in the episode the crate dragon a  huge worm-like creature  swallows one whole later a tuscan raider  brushes abanthus teeth and in the  climactic scene  a bantha is rigged with explosives and  used to bait the crate  the dragon swallows the bantha bomb and  the mandalorian detonates it causing  both animals to explode in a shower of  flame and flesh  in other news the aspca does not appear  to have a tatooine chapter  while it's true that banthas provide the  tuscan raiders with the means of  transportation across  tatooine's unforgiving terrain they  aren't merely beasts of burden  in fact every tuscan has a deep lifelong  connection with their bantha the  culture's initiation right which  typically takes place during youth  includes gifting the initiate a bantha  that matches their gender  when tuscans marry their banth is made  as well and in the unfortunate  circumstance that a bantha should die  before its rider  tradition calls for its remains to be  interred in a graveyard with sacred  meaning to the tuscan people  one of the star wars saga's strongest  points is the way in which  even incidental elements can be fully  fleshed out into stories of their own  and the bond between the banthas and the  sand people is a perfect example they  may have served as little more than a  brief  terrifying plot point in a new hope but  the hidden depths to their story  offer some of the franchise's most  compelling allegories  the tuscans have a cameo during the  episode 1 pod racing sequence and their  brief appearance in the movie echoes  their aggressive characterization from a  new hope  in the scene young anakin skywalker is  racing the villainous of bulba to gain  his freedom from slavery when the  tuscans attempt to disrupt the race  from their perch in the rocky hills  above the desert racetrack  the raiders take pot shots at the racers  with their long-barreled rifles  and celebrate when they hit anakin's pop  whether the tuscans are doing this for a  vicious sport or whether they feel they  are protecting their territory from  interlopers is never specified  either way the brief scene sets the  stage for the tuscan's longer appearance  in episode 2 a movie that seems to use  their established villainy to  justify their wholesale slaughter tuscan  raiders feature prominently in episode 2  attack of the clones in which they  kidnap and brutalize anakin's mother  shmi skywalker  anakin sneaks into their camp to  retrieve her but he is too late  and she dies in his arms in a fit of  vengeful rage he exterminates every  raider in the camp  including women and children this scene  appears to further equate the sand  people with cinematic native american  violence in lucas's vision  the kidnapping and slaughter and attack  of the clones again echoes the searchers  when the title characters discover  kidnapped white women in comanche tents  similar to the ones in episode 2. later  john wayne's character ethan  gleefully participates in a bloody  slaughter of the comanche which includes  scalping them  anakin also appears to relish his  butchery and tells padme that he simply  slaughtered them like  animals although lucas means to portray  anakin's killing as heartless a  significant step towards his embrace of  the dark  side the director's sympathies seem to  lie at least partially with anakin  anakin may be in the wrong but the  raiders are nevertheless depicted as  sub-creatures  undeserving of pity like most pop  culture properties that have been around  for decades  star wars has tried to keep up with the  times by reflecting social and cultural  changes  in particular the franchise has  attempted to be more diverse in its race  and gender representation  an effort that has resulted in backlash  from some fans who don't care  for change the mandalorian has been part  of this effort  both with its casting and behind the  camera it even attempts a makeover of  the tuscan raiders by representing them  as a less  overtly aggressive and more complex  culture the more complex portrait of the  sand people of the mandalorian  debuts in chapter five of season one  when another character implies that the  tuscans are filth  who bother the locals the mandalorian  responds  tuscans think they're the locals  everyone else is just trespassing  he then peacefully attains passage  through their land by negotiating with  them in their sign language  this is the first notion anywhere in  film star wars that raiders have a  language  let alone one that allows them to  communicate with other peoples language  is one of the most important markers of  civilization  and this episode presents it as a  feature of the tuscans who are no longer  depicted  as the backward savages of previous eras  after their cameo in chapter 5 of the  mandalorian the tuscans return in the  action-packed season 2 opener in an even  more vital role  in this episode the mandalorian enlists  their help to kill a crate dragon this  quest returns full circle to the saga's  introduction of the sand people in a new  hope when ben kenobi's crate noises  frighten them away from luke  but this time we see the actual beast  and quickly understand why the sand  people as well as the human settlers of  the frontier town  maz pelgo fear it consistent with the  mistrust dramatized between settlers and  natives in so many westerns the human  settlers in mos palgo are reluctant to  trust the sand people  until the mandalorian broke as a  tentative piece naturally  the mandalorian is fluent in two  separate dialects of tuscan  one silent one spoken and so is able to  explain that the nomads are not only  capable  and rational but also honorable people  who keep their word  working together the humans and tuscans  defeat their common enemy of course many  of the sand people die in the cause  while virtually all the humans walk away  unscathed  tuscan raiders may have come a long way  in the star wars universe but evidently  they are still expendable not much about  tuscan culture is seen or discussed in  the star wars movies but as with just  about any other species that's even so  much as glimpsed in the background of  the scene there's a deep well of  information about the raiders that star  wars fans can glean from a variety of  sources  indigenous to tatooine the tuscan  raiders not only view  outsiders with deep and often violent  mistrust but they regard natural  resources  particularly water with a spiritual  level of respect  it's this component of the tuscan belief  system that led them to raid moisture  farms and attack  travelers who wandered too close to the  planet's scarce natural wells  of course all any long time star wars  fan wants to know about the tuscan  raiders is what they look like under  their masks  a question that seems unlikely to ever  be answered in canonical media the  tuscans have an almost religious  aversion to removing them  only showing their faces during life  milestones such as their wedding night  the tuscan raiders speak using a  combination of vocal speech  and sign language and as we see in the  mandalorian our hero is well versed in  both forms  what casual viewers may not realize  however is that tuscan sign language is  real  and it was developed by actor troy  kotzer a mandalorian actor who is deaf  and developed the language to look as  little like american sign language as  possible  mandalorian viewers caught a good  glimpse of kotzer's work during the  season 2 episode the marshall  in which mando and cobb vanth team up  with the tuscan tribe to kill a crate  dragon that's been plaguing the tatooine  outpost of moss palgo  in late 1977 and early 1978 the first  year after the original film's release  star wars sold  100 million dollars worth of toys  industry-wide  sales are up by almost eight percent  most of the increase attributable to the  space toys craze  22 million figures per year  since then star wars toy merchandising  has become a billion dollar industry  still thriving today with new action  figures funko pops  and lego sets introduced regularly there  are even books and documentaries devoted  to the creation and selling of the toys  as well as many volumes and sites  cataloging them george lucas famously  negotiated the rights to the star wars  sequels from 20th century fox  partially so he could have the  merchandising rights as well one cynical  way of explaining the sheer number of  new characters and ships for each new  movie is by considering how many new  toys lucas could peddle along with their  more diminutive fellow tatooine dwellers  the jawas the sand people as they were  then known  were among the first dozen figures to  kick off the global merchandising  phenomenon  there have been many iterations of the  figures since some now going by their  proper tuscan rated designation  the original vintage action figure now a  collector's item can be had for hundreds  of dollars on ebay  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  star wars are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one

